.TH PRX 1 "2026-09-02" "prx" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prx \- TUI for open GitHub pull request status across repos and authors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prx
[\fB--config\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB--plain\fR | \fB--json\fR] [\fB--all\fR] [\fB--by\fR stack|repo|author|change]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prx
lists open GitHub pull requests for the repositories and authors defined in a
YAML config file in one flat table, sortable by repo or author. For each PR it
shows the repo, PR number, author, CI state, reviewers with their review
state, a recommended action, the head branch, and the title. The TUI uses Nerd
Font glyphs (octicons), GitHub-style: a pull-request/merge icon colored by
status (open green, draft grey, merged purple, closed red), a filled CI dot
(green/yellow/red, grey when no checks), per-reviewer check marks (green
approved, grey pending, red x for changes requested), and a branch icon.
A Nerd Font is recommended for the TUI. On first run prx asks whether the
sample glyphs render; the answer is saved as \fInerd_font\fR in the config. With
\fInerd_font: false\fR the TUI uses plain text instead (status \fB○ · ● ×\fR,
CI \fB✓ ✗ ~ ?\fR, filter labels led by their trigger key, decorative icons
dropped); toggle it anytime in the \fB,\fR settings. \fB--plain\fR and
\fB--json\fR output is always plain text. Data is fetched via the
.BR gh (1)
CLI (GraphQL API), which must be installed and authenticated.
.PP
Without flags it opens an interactive curses TUI. With
.B --plain
or
.B --json
it prints once to stdout and exits, which is the mode intended for scripts and
AI agents.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Config file to use. Default: \fI~/.config/prx/prx.yaml\fR.
.TP
.B --plain
Print the table to stdout instead of starting the TUI.
.TP
.B --json
Print all PRs and fetch errors as JSON to stdout instead of starting the TUI.
.TP
.B --all
Show PRs from all authors. By default, if authors are configured, only PRs by
those authors are shown.
.TP
.BI --state " all|open|merged|closed"
State filter for \fB--plain\fR/\fB--json\fR output. \fIopen\fR includes
drafts. Default: all. In the TUI, cycle with \fBs\fR instead.
.TP
.BI --author " name"
Only show PRs by this author (config short name or GitHub login) in
\fB--plain\fR/\fB--json\fR output. In the TUI, cycle through configured
authors with \fBa\fR instead.
.TP
.BI --window " day|week|month"
Only show PRs updated within the last 1/7/30 days, a moving window relative to
now. Default: week. In the TUI, cycle with \fBl\fR instead. Fetching always
covers a month, so the TUI toggle is instant; PRs with no activity for over 30
days are never shown.
.TP
.BI --by " stack|repo|author|change"
Sort for \fB--plain\fR output. \fIstack\fR sorts by repo and groups stacked
PRs under their root; \fIchange\fR sorts by last change, newest first.
Default: stack.
.TP
.BI --refresh " seconds"
TUI auto-sync interval; 0 disables. Overrides the \fIrefresh\fR config key.
Default: config value, else 300. Fetches run in the background; a spinner in
the title bar indicates activity while the current list stays on screen.
A sync only fetches what the current filters show (repo, state, last).
Widening a filter (state, last, repo) triggers a sync 3 seconds after the
last cycle keypress to fill in rows not yet fetched.
.TP
.B --clear-cache
Delete the TUI's PR cache (see CACHE) and exit.
.TP
.B --help
Show usage summary.
.SH CONFIGURATION
YAML file with two lists. \fIname\fR is the short display name; \fIurl\fR is
the GitHub repository URL; a repo's optional \fIicon\fR names a Nerd Font
glyph shown before its name in the TUI; \fItag\fR is the author's GitHub
login. Icon names cover common languages, frameworks, and tech (angular,
react, vue, nodejs, python, go, rust, docker, kubernetes, aws, postgres,
server, database, ...); the full list is in the comment at the bottom of the
example config. An unknown value is rendered verbatim, so a raw glyph also
works. Default: the GitHub repo octicon.
Optional top-level keys: \fIrefresh\fR sets the TUI auto-sync interval in
seconds (default 300, 0 disables); \fIrefresh_waiting\fR sets the fast
refresh interval for rows marked waiting with \fBw\fR (default 30, 0
disables). The TUI persists its filter state (org, last, sort, state,
repo, author, auto on/off) and the waiting marks to \fIstate.json\fR next to
the config file and restores them on start; \fIrestore_filters: false\fR
and/or \fIrestore_waiting: false\fR disable the hydration (saving always
happens).
.PP
.nf
refresh: 300
refresh_waiting: 30
repos:
  - name: backend
    url: https://github.com/acme/backend
  - name: web
    url: https://github.com/acme/web-app
authors:
  - name: robin
    tag: robin-gh-login
  - name: quinn
    tag: quinn-codes
.fi
.SH COLUMNS
.TP
.B REPO
Configured short name of the repository.
.TP
.B PR
Pull request number.
.TP
.B AUTHOR
Author short name from \fIauthors\fR, else the GitHub login. Your own PRs
(the entry with \fIme: true\fR) show in blue.
.TP
.B STATUS
draft, open, merged, or closed.
.TP
.B REVIEW
(\fB--plain\fR/\fB--json\fR)
.B pending
(review required),
.B reviewed
(approved), or
.B changes
(changes requested).
.TP
.B REV
TUI: three fixed slots at the same position in every row, color-coded.
Verdict: red thumb-down with the change-requester count if anyone requested
changes (\fB-N\fR without a nerd font), else green thumb-up with the approval
count (\fB+N\fR; approvers are never named, your own approval included).
Pending: person-with-clock with the number of open review requests, people and
teams together (\fB?N\fR; grey, orange when any is a team or an outsider — not
a configured author; teams would need the read:org scope to be named). A zero
count shows dark grey (\fB+0\fR, \fB?0\fR). Last, \fBme\fR with your own
state when you are a reviewer: eye when your review is requested (\fB?me\fR,
blue highlight), thumb-down when you requested changes (\fB-me\fR, red),
thumb-up when you approved (\fB+me\fR, green). "You" is the author entry with
\fIme: true\fR; you are never counted in the change or pending slots. Counts
cap at 9. Example: \fB+2 ?1 ?me\fR. The detail card names every reviewer.
Plain/JSON: all reviewers with suffix \fB+\fR approved, \fB?\fR pending,
\fB-\fR changes requested; JSON carries \fIdecision\fR and \fIext_review\fR.
.TP
.B DAYS
Days since the PR was created, e.g. \fB3d\fR, \fB21d\fR (capped at 99d).
.TP
.B CHNG
Time since the PR last changed (GitHub \fIupdatedAt\fR: commits, reviews,
comments, state changes): hours under a day (\fB0h\fR\(en\fB23h\fR), then days
like \fBDAYS\fR (\fB1d\fR, capped at \fB99d\fR). The detail card's
\fIupdated … ago\fR uses the same format. This is the timestamp the
time window filters on. Hidden in compact view.
.TP
.B BRANCH
Head branch of the PR.
.TP
.B CI
TUI: one icon for the loudest state of the head commit's checks — red filled
✗-circle if any failed, yellow radio dot if any running, grey hollow circle if
only queued, grey cancel sign if a check was cancelled (CI never finished), green
outlined ✓-circle if all passed, grey \- when no checks.
Plain/JSON: the combined state as GREEN/YELLOW/RED/CANCELLED (\- when no
checks), plus a \fIchecks\fR field [passed, failed, running, queued, cancelled]
in JSON. A cancelled check is no result, not a failure: CANCELLED unless
something failed or still runs. A check that ran
more than once on the same commit (a re-run, a label event) counts only with
its newest run: per GitHub Actions workflow only its newest run counts (a run
cancelled before its matrix expanded has different job names), then per
check name its newest start \(em the way GitHub's PR page shows it; GitHub's own combined status still counts the stale runs, so a PR whose
old run failed and whose re-run passed is green here and on the PR page.
.TP
.B ACTION
The next move (icon + word in the TUI), first match wins:
.B review
= your review is requested (highlighted; requires \fIme: true\fR);
.B rebase
= merge conflict;
.B fix ci
= a check failed;
.B rerun
= CI was cancelled and nothing failed: no code to fix, the run has to run again
(\fBM\fR re-runs the cancelled jobs with \fBgh run rerun --failed\fR after a
\fBy\fR confirm; it starts CI others see and that costs Actions minutes);
.B address
= changes requested;
.B ready
= still a draft: mark it ready for review;
.B update
= its chain or stack is out of date where it matters: a layer is behind its
parent PR's branch (a lower layer moved), or GitHub reports it \fIBEHIND\fR
(the repository requires up-to-date branches). Every layer of that chain says
update instead of \fIland\fR, \fIstack\fR, \fIhold\fR, \fIblocked\fR,
\fImerge\fR, \fIwait\fR or \fIchase\fR (\fBM\fR updates it; see MERGING);
never while a layer of the chain has a merge conflict. Being behind only the
default branch is not an action \(em see the detail card and \fBM\fR;
.B merge
= approved, CI green, clean, based on the default branch — go merge
(\fBM\fR);
.B land
= any layer of a native GitHub stack whose unmerged layers are all approved,
green, not drafts and not conflicting, none reported \fIBLOCKED\fR by GitHub
(e.g. a code-owner review that a layer's files need \(em the stack merge applies
the base branch's rules to every layer), the bottom one clean (\fBM\fR on any of
them lands the whole stack in one go); while one layer isn't ready, no layer
says land and the bottom one merges like any PR;
.B stack
= a plain stack (PRs chained by base branch, not yet a native GitHub stack)
and \fBgh-stack\fR is installed: every ready layer that would otherwise say
\fImerge\fR (the bottom) or \fIhold\fR (the layers above) says stack instead,
because merging the bottom alone forces rebases above it (\fBM\fR links the
whole chain; see MERGING). Only for the squash and rebase merge methods: with
merge commits the layers above stay valid, so the chain keeps
\fImerge\fR/\fIhold\fR. Not for trees: when a PR has two open PRs on its
branch, those rows keep \fImerge\fR/\fIhold\fR;
.B hold
= otherwise mergeable, but stacked on another open PR's branch: merge that one
first (without \fBgh-stack\fR installed);
.B blocked
= otherwise mergeable, but based on a branch that is neither the default branch
nor another PR's: merge in the browser;
.B wait
= CI running, nothing to do yet;
.B chase
= only reviews are missing, go ping;
.B done
= merged/closed.
.SH STACKED PRS
A PR whose base branch is the head branch of another visible PR is part of a
stack. In the default \fIstack\fR sort, members are grouped directly under
their root and marked in a purple rail column left of the PR number:
\fB▪\fR root, \fB├\fR member, \fB└\fR last member. In repo/author sort, rows keep pure sort order and every
stack member shows a plain \fB▪\fR instead. Native GitHub stacks keep the solid
purple rail; plain stacks (not linked yet, see the \fIstack\fR action) show a
dashed grey one: \fB▫\fR, \fB┆\fR, \fB╰\fR. If the parent is not visible
(merged, filtered out), the child appears as a normal row. \fB--json\fR output
carries the \fIbase\fR branch per PR.
.SH TUI KEYS
Every cycling key cycles backwards with Shift (O, S, R, A, L, B). The
footer shows only the essentials; \fB?\fR opens a modal listing every key with
a short description. It closes on \fBq q\fR only, like quitting; pressing any
other key highlights the line that key belongs to for about half a second, so
the modal doubles as a key trainer. A shifted cycle key (\fBShift+r\fR and
friends) highlights two lines: its own and the \fBShift\fR line. Auto sync
and the waiting schedule keep running while it is open.
.P
The footer's right end shows the most practical key for the selected row,
always as the key (never an icon): \fBM\fR with what it does (\fImerge\fR,
\fIland\fR or \fIstack\fR) when \fBM\fR acts on the row; else \fBw\fR
\fIwatch\fR while its CI is running and it isn't watched yet; else
\fB↵\fR \fIdetails\fR.
.TP
.B q q
Quit — pressed twice within 2 seconds. The first press shows a
"press q again to quit" prompt in the footer; any other key withdraws it.
Ctrl+C quits immediately.
.TP
.B Esc
Sync everything the current filters show.
.TP
.B 0
Reset state, last, repo, author, search, and sort to their defaults —
the org filter is kept.
.TP
.B o
Cycle the organization filter: all, then each organization derived from the
configured repo URLs. With "all" selected and more than one organization
configured, the table is split into one section per organization, each with
its own column header and PR count. Switching to an organization clears a
repo filter from another organization.
.TP
.B s
Cycle the state filter: all, open (includes drafts), merged, closed. No
refetch needed; merged/closed rows show \fB-\fR as action.
.TP
.B r
Cycle the repo filter: all, then each configured repository \(em only the
selected organization's repositories when the org filter is set.
.TP
.B a
Cycle the author filter: all, then each configured author.
.TP
.B l
Cycle the last window: day, week, month (PRs updated in the last 1/7/30 days).
.TP
.B b
Cycle the sort: stack (default), repo, author, change (last change, newest
first \(em what just moved on top).
.TP
.B /
Fuzzy search by PR number, branch, and title. The search operates on exactly
the rows the current filters show — it never fetches or widens anything;
widen the last window with \fBl\fR first if needed. Matching adapts to input
length: digit and ticket queries under 3
digits match as prefixes (typing never blanks the table); from 3 digits on,
\fI116\fR / \fIMIT-116\fR / \fImit116\fR matches exactly that ticket reference
(never ABC-1160 or ABC-161) or a PR number prefix; anything else is a
case-insensitive substring match. Matched text is highlighted in the rows
(config \fIsearch_highlight: false\fR disables it), and the selection jumps to
the best-scoring match while typing (exact PR number beats exact ticket beats
prefix; \fIsearch_select_best: false\fR disables it). Typing edits
the query live; \fBEnter\fR keeps the filter, \fBEsc\fR clears it; \fB/\fR
re-edits a kept filter. \fB0\fR (reset) also clears it.
.TP
.B ,
Open the settings modal: edit \fIrefresh\fR, \fIrefresh_waiting\fR,
\fIrestore_filters\fR, \fIrestore_waiting\fR, \fIsearch_highlight\fR, and two
entries opening sub-modals — \fIrepos\fR and \fIauthors\fR. Changes are written
to the config file immediately; the refresh intervals apply live. Enter edits a
number (digits, enter commits, esc cancels), space/enter toggles a boolean, esc
closes.
.IP
\fIrepos\fR opens the repo import modal, the only way in. Navigation is drill-in
with the arrow keys: right goes deeper, left goes back. Pick an organization
(or your own account), then check/uncheck its repositories with \fBspace\fR.
Right arrow on a repo row focuses its cells: first the 2-char short name (type
to overwrite), right again the icon (up/down steps through the named icons,
typing filters them). Short name and icon are prefilled from the repo name and
its primary language. \fBEnter\fR at row level writes the selection to the
config file (checked repos of that org are added or updated, unchecked ones
removed; other orgs untouched) and refetches. \fBEsc\fR cancels.
.IP
\fIauthors\fR manages author entries: short name, GitHub login tag, and the
\fIme\fR flag; space sets me exclusively, n adds, x deletes, enter saves to the
config, esc cancels.
.TP
.B c
Toggle compact columns: only status, PR number, CI, branch, title, and action.
Terminals narrower than 95 columns switch to the compact view automatically;
the title column always shrinks and grows with the remaining space, all other
columns are fixed width.
.TP
.B h
Hold for hints: while \fBh\fR is held, every filter label and footer item
shows the key that triggers it instead of its icon, reverting about half a
second after release. Uses terminal key-repeat, so it works over ssh/mosh.
\fIshow_filter_keys: true\fR in the config shows the keys permanently (default
false; also in the \fB,\fR settings modal).
.TP
.B Loading line
While a fetch for a wider scope runs (start-up, or a filter change that needs
data not loaded yet, including the 3 s debounce before it starts), a dark grey
line under the last row shows a spinner, the scope, and the repos still in
flight, e.g. \fI⠼ loading month · FE, DE\fR. Plain syncs don't show it.
.TP
.B Tab
Toggle auto sync on/off (the top bar shows \fIauto\fR or \fImanual\fR).
Next to it, \fIlast sync 42s ago\fR gives the age of the last completed
fetch. One unit, largest that fits: \fBs\fR under a minute, then \fBm\fR,
\fBh\fR, \fBd\fR, \fBw\fR, capped at \fB54w\fR.
Manual mode still allows \fBEsc\fR and does not affect the waiting schedule.
.TP
.B w
Toggle "waiting" on the selected PR: waiting PRs are refreshed individually on
the fast \fIrefresh_waiting\fR schedule (default every 30 seconds) in addition
to the sync. Each waiting tick also fetches the newest open PRs per
repo in scope, so newly opened PRs show up between syncs; rows drop
out on their own once they age past the time window. The PR number turns purple while waiting is on.
A waiting mark clears itself as soon as the PR is merged or closed.
.TP
.B Space
Refresh the selected PR only, leaving the rest of the list untouched. The row
shows the fetch spinner while it runs. No-op while a sync of that
repository is already in flight.
.TP
.B M
Merge, land, stack, update or re-run the selected PR, after a fresh check and
a \fBy\fR confirm; see MERGING. Also in the detail card. \fBM\fR does what the
action says when it is \fImerge\fR, \fIland\fR, \fIstack\fR, \fIupdate\fR or
\fIrerun\fR; on any other row of
a plain chain (with \fBgh-stack\fR installed; not a tree) it links the chain,
whatever the action says \(em a chain can be linked before it is reviewed;
and on a row whose chain is only behind the default branch it updates it
(see MERGING). Nothing is offered while a layer of the chain has a conflict.
The footer's right end shows what \fBM\fR does on the selected row, e.g.
\fBM\fR \fIstack\fR (see TUI KEYS for the other hints there).
.TP
.B y / Y
Yank (copy to the clipboard) the selected PR's number (\fB#123\fR) or link.
The footer confirms with \fIcopied …\fR. Uses \fBpbcopy\fR, \fBwl-copy\fR or
\fBxclip\fR, whichever exists. Also works in the detail card.
.TP
.B j / k / arrows
Move selection. When rows don't fit, the first or last visible line turns into
a dark grey \fI↑ N more\fR / \fI↓ N more\fR marker for the rows hidden in that
direction; the list scrolls so the selection never sits under a marker.
.TP
.B Enter
Open a detail card for the selected PR. Sections, in order:
about (base/head branch, plus \fIbehind N\fR when the base has N commits the
head lacks; repo/org, author, age), status, stack (only when the
PR is part of a stack; current row marked), reviews (decision and per-reviewer
state), comments (review threads: open, resolved, total \(em plain
conversation comments can't be resolved and are not counted), CI check counts, and action (the next move with its reason). All from data already fetched. \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR
or the arrow keys move to the previous/next row without leaving the card.
\fBEnter\fR opens the PR in the browser (the card stays open), \fBSpace\fR
refetches the PR the card is showing, \fBw\fR toggles
waiting for it (the number turns purple), \fBM\fR merges, lands or stacks it
(see MERGING; only shown when \fBM\fR does something on the PR), and
\fBEsc\fR closes (keeping the row you landed on selected). The card updates live as watch or refresh data
arrives; a spinner in the top-left border marks a fetch in flight.
.SH MERGING
\fBM\fR (main view or detail card) refetches the PR and recomputes its action; only a
fresh \fImerge\fR, \fIland\fR or \fIstack\fR (see \fBM\fR under TUI KEYS)
continues. A confirm dialog then shows the
merge method (the repository's \fIviewerDefaultMergeMethod\fR, e.g.
\fIsquash into development\fR), whether the branch will be deleted (the
repository's \fIdeleteBranchOnMerge\fR), a merge queue note, and warnings:
PRs that are not yours (the author entry with \fIme: true\fR), and plain
stacked children that need \fBgit rebase --onto\fR afterwards. \fBy\fR
confirms, any other key cancels.
.P
\fImerge\fR runs \fBgh pr merge\fR with \fB--match-head-commit\fR pinned to
the refetched head, so a push after the check makes the merge fail instead of
merging unseen commits. \fB--admin\fR is never used.
.P
\fIland\fR runs \fBgh stack merge\fR (the \fBgh-stack\fR extension) with
\fBGH_REPO\fR set and the stack number, merging every unmerged layer of the
stack in one all-or-nothing operation, whichever layer \fBM\fR started on. Without the extension a dialog offers \fBb\fR (merge
the bottom layer instead) and \fBc\fR (copy
\fBgh extension install github/gh-stack\fR); any other key cancels.
.P
\fIstack\fR walks the chain live on GitHub, so rows hidden by the time window
or filters still count: down through each PR's base branch to the first PR
whose base has no open PR, then up through the open PRs based on each head
branch. A PR with two open PRs on its branch is a tree, which a stack can't
hold: prx refuses and names it. The confirm dialog lists the chain bottom to
top with its base, PRs already in a stack (\fBlink\fR adds the rest to it),
PRs that are not yours, and drafts (they stay drafts). \fBy\fR runs
\fBgh stack link --base\fR \fIbottom's base\fR with the PR URLs and
\fBGH_REPO\fR set: URLs never resolve as branch names (which would be pushed)
or stack numbers, and the pinned base keeps \fBlink\fR from retargeting the
bottom PR to the default branch. Every layer is refreshed afterwards, so the
rows turn into \fIland\fR once they are ready.
.P
The bottom of a chain offers the other path as a one-key escape. When the
stack dialog opens on a ready bottom whose action is \fIstack\fR, \fBm\fR
merges only that PR (the merge dialog follows, with its \fIrebase --onto\fR
warning). When the merge dialog opens on a chain bottom that can be linked
(merge-commit repositories keep \fImerge\fR there), \fBs\fR links the chain
instead.
.P
\fIupdate\fR brings the chain through the PR up to date, bottom to top, with
GitHub's update-branch operation (\fBupdatePullRequestBranch\fR). Chains
and stacks are always rebased: GitHub's stacked PRs rely on rebasing, and a
merge commit in a layer leaves the stack needing \fBgh stack rebase\fR. A
single PR gets a merge commit, unless the repository lands with rebase. The
dialog says which, and warns that a rebase rewrites every branch, naming PRs
that are not yours. It walks the chain live like \fIstack\fR, refuses
up front when a layer has a merge conflict (showing the local fix, see below), and waits for each layer to
stop being behind before updating the next one (GitHub does not restack the
layers above by itself). The first failure stops the run, without rollback;
the result names what was updated, what failed and why, and what was left
untouched. Every layer is refreshed afterwards. prx can't resolve a merge
conflict: when one blocks the update (up front or partway), a dialog shows
the commands to fix it locally \(em \fBgh stack checkout\fR \fIN\fR,
\fBgh stack rebase\fR, fix, \fBgh stack push\fR for a native stack, a plain
\fBgit rebase\fR otherwise \(em and \fBc\fR copies them. The detail card of
a row whose action is \fIrebase\fR lists the same commands. The merge, land and stack
dialogs offer \fBu\fR (update first) whenever the chain is behind and has no
conflict, so any open PR can be updated. Behind counts come from one
\fBcompare\fR request per repository per sync (and per refresh);
PRs from forks are not checked.
.SH CACHE
The TUI keeps one JSON file per repository in
\fI${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/prx/\fR (named by the SHA-1 of
\fIowner/repo\fR). At start-up it restores the cached rows at once — the top
bar reads \fIrestored 2h ago\fR until the first sync finishes — then syncs.
Merged PRs are final, so MERGED is delta-synced: paging stops at the last
sync (minus 5 minutes) when an earlier sync already covered the window.
OPEN and CLOSED always get a full sync, because CI results and merge state
change without touching a PR's \fIupdatedAt\fR. Entries older than 30 days are
pruned; a query change (new fields) discards the old files automatically.
\fB--plain\fR and \fB--json\fR never read or write the cache. Clear it with
\fBprx --clear-cache\fR or \fIclear cache\fR in the \fB,\fR settings modal
(which starts a full sync). Deleting the folder by hand is always safe.
Terms (sync, full sync, delta sync, refresh, restore): see \fIGLOSSARY.md\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/prx/prx.yaml
Configuration (see \fB--config\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.config/prx/state.json
Filters and waiting marks, next to the config file.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/prx/*.json
PR cache, one file per repository; disposable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a repo failed to fetch in \fB--plain\fR mode, or on config
errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Interactive TUI:
.B prx
.TP
Machine-readable snapshot for an agent:
.B prx --json
.TP
Everyone's PRs sorted by author:
.B prx --plain --all --by author
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gh (1)
